Requirements: 27   with obligations: 21   deliberately none: 6

[task-01] Obligation derivation reads one requirement at a time and produces obligations
    for it alone, so a requirement stated in two places yields two obligations that
    assert the same thing. On one observed task file, 34 requirements produced 71
    obligations. Every later stage runs once per obligation, so the duplication
    multiplies the work and the length of the report while adding nothing.
    -> duplication-per-requirement  [functional/explicit]
       Derive obligations one requirement at a time so that a requirement stated in two
       places produces two obligations that each assert the same thing.
    -> later-stages-per-obligation  [invariant/explicit]
       Run every later stage once per obligation, so duplicated obligations multiply
       downstream work and report length.

[task-02] The duplication is not a fault in the input. A mandate and its acceptance
    criteria naturally restate each other, and a requirement is often followed by a
    clause giving the reason for it. Both are ordinary ways to write a requirement, and
    both currently produce a second obligation.
    -> duplication-is-ordinary-restatement  [functional/explicit]
       Treat ordinary restatements such as a mandate and its acceptance criteria, or a
       requirement followed by its reason, as separate requirement statements that
       currently produce a second obligation.
    -> duplication-not-input-fault  [invariant/explicit]
       Preserve the interpretation that the duplication arises from ordinary requirement
       wording rather than from a fault in the input.

[task-03] Add a pass that runs after derivation and recognises when two obligations
    state the same requirement. Its output is a link rather than a deletion: the
    surviving obligation is named by every requirement that stated it.
    -> post-derivation-duplicate-linking  [functional/explicit]
       Add a post-derivation pass that recognises when two obligations state the same
       requirement and outputs a link instead of deleting either obligation.
    -> surviving-obligation-named-by-all-requirements  [invariant/explicit]
       Ensure the surviving obligation is named by every requirement that stated it.

[constraint-01] The pass runs after obligation derivation, over the obligations
    derivation produced, rather than inside derivation.
    -> post-derivation-linking-pass  [functional/explicit]
       Run the duplicate-recognition pass after obligation derivation, operating on the
       obligations derivation output rather than inside derivation.

[constraint-02] Recognising a duplicate produces a link from a second requirement to an
    existing obligation. No obligation is deleted and no requirement text is discarded.
    -> duplicate-recognition-links-existing-obligation  [functional/explicit]
       Represent a recognized duplicate as a link from the later requirement to an
       existing obligation while preserving both the obligation and the requirement
       text.

[constraint-03] A linked obligation carries the union of the requirement links and the
    union of the source spans of everything merged into it, so it still traces to every
    piece of task text that produced it.
    -> linked-obligation-preserves-union-of-provenance  [invariant/explicit]
       Carry forward the union of requirement links and the union of source spans from
       everything merged into a linked obligation.

[constraint-04] The links are typed fields in the schema-constrained response. A link
    stated in an obligation's description, rationale, or any other free-text field does
    not count as a link.
    -> typed-link-fields-only  [compatibility/explicit]
       Represent links as typed schema fields, so only schema-typed link fields count as
       links and free-text mentions do not.

[constraint-05] Two obligations about the same area of the change are not necessarily
    the same requirement. Where the judgement is uncertain, leave them separate.
    -> uncertain-duplicates-remain-separate  [human_review/explicit]
       Preserve separate obligations when duplicate recognition is uncertain, even if
       the obligations concern the same area of the change.

[constraint-06] A requirement and a clause giving the reason for that requirement state
    one requirement, not two.
    -> reason-clause-counts-as-same-requirement-2  [functional/explicit]
       Treat a requirement and its reason clause as one requirement for duplicate
       recognition.

[constraint-07] The obligations derivation produced, before any linking, are persisted
    in the review state. They are provenance rather than report content.
    -> prelink-derivation-persisted-in-review-state  [invariant/explicit]   (also serves completion-05)
       The obligations derivation produced before linking is present in the persisted
       review state.

[constraint-08] The links this pass produces are identical unless the derived
    obligations they were computed from change.
    -> stable-links-unless-derived-obligations-change  [regression/explicit]
       Keep produced links identical whenever the derived obligations they were computed
       from are unchanged.

[constraint-09] Two runs over byte-identical task text produce byte-identical review
    state, both for the derived obligations and for the linked result.
    -> byte-identical-review-state-for-identical-input  [regression/explicit]   (also serves completion-07)
       Two runs over byte-identical task text produce byte-identical review state at
       both the derived and the linked stage.

[constraint-10] Typed schemas are pydantic models, as the rest of the repository defines
    them.
    -> typed-schemas-pydantic-models  [compatibility/explicit]
       Represent the typed schemas as pydantic models.

[constraint-11] Tests issue no live model calls.
    -> tests-no-live-model-calls  [invariant/explicit]
       Keep tests free of live model calls.

